  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis\n\nThis codebase is unusually disciplined already — most of the classic mistakes (raw N+1 loops, missing `useEffect` cleanup, uncontrolled polling) have been deliberately designed around, with comments explaining the tradeoffs. I read through the full server data layer (`lib/server/*`), schema, all client components, and the DB migrations rather than guessing. Here's what's actually there, ranked by impact.\n\n### 1. Missing indexes on foreign-key columns (real, highest impact)\n\nPostgres does **not** auto-index foreign keys. Checking `lib/db/schema.ts` against the actual migrations (`drizzle/0000_organic_black_panther.sql`, `0002_add_source_credentials.sql`), the only indexes in the entire database are:\n\n```\nentities_run_source_external_idx  ON entities (run_id, source_id, external_id)\nsource_credentials_source_idx     ON source_credentials (source_id)\n```\n\nEvery other foreign key is unindexed, including the columns that are filtered or joined on in nearly every query in the app:\n\n- `conflicts.runId` — filtered in `getQueuePage`, `getSeverityBreakdown`, `countFiltered`, `getQueueCounts`, `loadDecisions`, `loadOrphans`, `countConflicts` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `lib/server/runs.ts`, `lib/server/export.ts`). Every one of these does a **sequential scan of the entire `conflicts` table**, not just the current run's rows.\n- `resolutions.conflictId` — left-joined in `baseQuery()` on essentially every conflicts page/route. The code even flags this itself in a comment: *\"not yet enforced by a unique index on `resolutions.conflict_id`\"* — worth fixing both for correctness (duplicate rows) and for the join cost.\n- `auditLog.runId` — filtered in `listRunAudit`, `getAuditPage`, `listAuditForExport`.\n- `matches.entityAId` / `matches.entityBId` / `matches.runId`.\n- `entities.parentExternalId` combined with `runId` — used by `countChildrenByParent`, which is called on *every* conflict-queue page load and every export. The existing composite index `(run_id, source_id, external_id)` doesn't serve this query at all (it filters on `run_id` + `parent_external_id`, not `source_id`/`external_id`).\n\nThis costs nothing today with a handful of demo rows, but it's the one thing here that will actually degrade as runs accumulate — and unlike the React-side items below, it gets worse silently (every conflicts-page load re-scans the whole table).\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts — add:\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { /* ...unchanged... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { /* ...unchanged... */ }, (table) => [\n  uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId), // also fixes the double-row risk the code already calls out\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { /* ...unchanged... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { /* ...unchanged... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const entities = pgTable(\"entities\", { /* ...unchanged... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"entities_run_source_external_idx\").on(table.runId, table.sourceId, table.externalId),\n  index(\"entities_run_parent_idx\").on(table.runId, table.parentExternalId), // new — serves countChildrenByParent\n]);\n```\nThen `npx drizzle-kit generate` to produce the migration.\n\n### 2. Conflict queue re-renders every row on every keystroke\n\n`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx` maps over up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE` (100) rows on every render, and `selectedIndex` state changes on every `j`/`k` keypress (`ConflictDenseTable`, `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow`, `ConflictDiffRow`). None of those row components are wrapped in `React.memo`, and `submitFor(row.conflictId)` (`conflict-queue.tsx:180`) creates a brand-new closure per row on every parent render regardless. So moving the selection down a 100-row page re-renders and re-diffs all 100 rows — including the diff computation in `ConflictSides`/`DiffValue` for every row — even though only two rows visually changed.\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }: Props) {\n  // ...unchanged body\n});\n```\nDo the same for `ListRow` in `conflict-focus.tsx` and the row `<button>` block in `conflict-dense-table.tsx` (pull it into its own component first). For `memo` to actually pay off, `submit` needs a stable identity per row — cache the per-row closures instead of recreating them inline:\n\n```ts\n// conflict-queue.tsx\nconst submitCache = useRef(new Map<string, ReviewSubmit>());\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit => {\n    let fn = submitCache.current.get(conflictId);\n    if (!fn) {\n      fn = (action) => submit(conflictId, action);\n      submitCache.current.set(conflictId, fn);\n    }\n    return fn;\n  },\n  [submit],\n);\n```\nThis only matters once page size grows or `ConflictSides`/`DiffValue` do more work — at 100 rows of plain string diffing it's not causing visible jank today, but it's the one real re-render inefficiency in the app and it's easy to fix cheaply.\n\n### 3. `RulesetEditor` — O(n²) per render (minor, real)\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`:\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);              // O(n) per row\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}         // O(n) per row, again\n      ...\n```\n`orderRulesByEvaluation(rules)` also runs unmemoized on every render. With rulesets in the tens of rows this is invisible, but it's redundant computation exactly as asked about — trivial to fix and removes the smell:\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst originalIndex = useMemo(\n  () => new Map(rules.map((rule, i) => [rule.id, i])),\n  [rules],\n);\n// then: const index = originalIndex.get(rule.id)!;\n```\n\n### What I did *not* find (worth stating explicitly, since you asked for all five categories)\n\n- **N+1 query patterns**: none. `lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `entities.ts`, `export.ts` are all written specifically to avoid this — `countChildrenByParent` batches child counts for a whole page in one grouped query, `getQueuePage` fires 3 queries in `Promise.all` rather than per-row, and the file comments explicitly call out \"three round trips, not three hundred.\" The only gap is the missing indexes above, not query shape.\n- **Memory leaks**: none found. `RunLive`'s polling `setInterval`/`useQuery` cleans up via TanStack Query's own lifecycle and stops polling once terminal; `GradientWaves` (WebGL/`ogl`) correctly cancels its animation frame, disconnects its `ResizeObserver`, removes the canvas, and releases the GL context on unmount; `LogoLoop`'s `ResizeObserver` disconnects; `ConflictFocus`'s keyboard listener and `useEffect`s all return cleanup functions.\n- **Caching**: `listSources`/`listRulesets`/`getActiveRuleset` hit Postgres on every page load rather than being cached — but this is explicitly intentional (`sources.ts:15-18`: *\"Configured sources are request-time state, not build-time\"*), and each is called at most once per request today, so there's no duplicate work to dedupe. I'd leave this alone rather than add caching that fights the app's own stated design (TanStack Query is deliberately scoped to run-status polling only, per `query-provider.tsx`).\n\n**Net recommendation**: add the five indexes in §1 — that's the one change here with real, compounding payoff as data grows. §2 and §3 are legitimate but low-urgency at current scale.\n",
